@Transactional
void demo() {
  Author a = new Author("A");          // @GeneratedValue(IDENTITY)
  em.persist(a);
  System.out.println("after persist");
  a.setName("B");
  em.flush();
  System.out.println("after flush");
}
INSERT, then after persist, then UPDATE, then after flush. IDENTITY forces the INSERT during persist, because Hibernate needs the id. The rename is seen by dirty checking. With SEQUENCE the order is: a nextval call, after persist, then at flush a single INSERT that already contains "B" with no UPDATE, as the queued insert reads the entity's current state.

Strategy | Entity can change? | Consistency | Cost | Fits
READ_ONLY | No, updates throw | Perfect, data never changes | Lowest | Countries, code lists
NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE | Yes | Short window of stale reads | Low, no locks | Rarely updated, staleness acceptable
READ_WRITE | Yes | Strong, uses soft locks | Medium | Read-mostly data that is updated
TRANSACTIONAL | Yes | Cache joins the JTA transaction | Highest | Only with a transactional cache provider
@Entity
@Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)
class Department { ... }
Rule of thumb: READ_ONLY for reference data, READ_WRITE for everything else you cache. If you cannot tolerate even brief staleness on a frequently updated entity, do not cache it.

